# Use Odroe in your Flutter app

Install Flutter 3.38.1 or newer with stable Dart 3.10.0 or newer. Keep the
official Flutter SDK and its bundled Dart together.

The `0.1.0-dev.1` framework preview has a different API from the former
`odroe` 0.0.8 UI package. Read the repository's MIGRATION.md before upgrading.

## Add Odroe to your Flutter app

Use an existing Flutter application, or start with an empty one:

```sh
flutter create --empty --platforms web my_app
cd my_app
flutter pub add 'odroe:{"version":"0.1.0-dev.1"}'
```

Replace `lib/main.dart` with this application:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/widgets.dart';
import 'package:odroe/odroe_flutter.dart';
import 'package:odroe/query_flutter.dart';
import 'package:odroe/router_flutter.dart';

final greeting = QueryOptions<String>(
  key: QueryKey<String>('welcome'),
  policy: const QueryPolicy(
    freshness: QueryFreshness.staleAfter(Duration(minutes: 5)),
  ),
  query: (_) async => 'Hello from Odroe',
);

final home = AppRoute<NoParams, NoSearch, NoData>(path: '/').page(
  build: (_) => Center(
    child: QueryBuilder<String>(
      options: greeting,
      builder: (_, result) =>
          Text(result.hasData ? result.requireData : 'Loading'),
    ),
  ),
);

final about = AppRoute<NoParams, NoSearch, NoData>(
  path: '/about',
).page(build: (_) => const Center(child: Text('About this app')));

Widget createApp() => App(
  modules: [
    QueryModule(),
    RouterModule(routes: [home, about]),
  ],
  builder: (app) => WidgetsApp.router(
    color: const Color(0xff222222),
    routerConfig: app.read(routerKey),
  ),
);

void main() => runApp(createApp());
```

Run and build it with the standard Flutter commands:

```sh
flutter run -d chrome
flutter build web --release
```

Adding the dependency and importing the APIs is enough. This application uses
App, Query, and manually declared routes without `odroe init`, generated files,
or an `odroe.yaml` configuration. Select only the modules your application
needs; Query and routing can also be used through their individual entrypoints.

Query owns the asynchronous greeting and its cache. RouterModule accepts the
two route objects directly. A server, database, or RPC origin is only needed
when your application uses those capabilities.

## Optional full-stack starter

For a generated Query/RPC/SQLite application, run the initializer in a separate,
empty Flutter application after adding Odroe:

```sh
dart run odroe init --full-stack
dart run odroe dev -- -d chrome
```

`init` is an optional template tool. It preserves custom application source and
refuses conflicts rather than replacing it. Plain `dart run odroe init` writes
a smaller Document + Router starter.

The next CLI revision can also create the entire application. From this
checkout with dependencies resolved:

```sh
dart run odroe create ../my_app --platforms web
```

By default, `create` pins the hosted dependency to the exact version of the CLI
running the command. It does not select `latest`. Override the version with
`--version 0.1.0-dev.1`, or use `--odroe-path .` for a checkout dependency; the
two overrides cannot be combined. The hosted package's installed CLI supplies
its initializer and generated routes. If that exact version is unavailable,
creation fails without publishing an incomplete application.

Hosted creation is not available in the published `0.1.0-dev.1` CLI; the
add-dependency path above works with that release today. For source development:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/odroe/odroe.git
cd odroe
flutter pub get
dart run odroe create ../my_app --odroe-path .
cd ../my_app
```

`create` requires a nonexistent target and stages the Flutter application beside
it before publication. Failed or interrupted stages remove only that private
directory and preserve existing targets. Use `--platforms`, `--org`, or
`--project-name` to customize the scaffold. `--offline` requires the selected
package and all its dependencies in the local cache.

File-based routing and automatic RPC codecs use generated Dart source. For
those features, `odroe generate` runs the compiler explicitly; `odroe dev` and
`odroe build` run it as part of their workflow. Ordinary Flutter commands do
not generate file-route manifests. Handwritten routes need no generation.

## Know what was created

```text
my_app/
├── lib/
│   ├── main.dart              # one-import Flutter composition root
│   ├── posts.dart             # client-safe post, page, and input records
│   ├── posts_database.dart    # shared schema and typed query builder
│   ├── rpc_origin.dart        # browser/native origin policy
│   ├── server.dart            # conditional server export
│   ├── server_native.dart     # process-owned SQLite
│   ├── server_cloudflare.dart # invocation-scoped D1
│   ├── routes/
│   │   ├── route.dart         # shared route and semantic document
│   │   ├── page.dart          # infinite query and create mutation
│   │   └── server.dart        # typed cursor-page/create RPC + SQL
│   ├── routes.dart            # generated client references
│   └── routes.server.dart     # generated server manifest
├── migrations/
│   ├── 0001_posts.sql         # create and seed
│   └── 0002_unify_posts.sql   # evolve data and add an index
├── odroe.yaml                  # SQLite source selected for dev/build
├── package.json
├── package-lock.json
└── wrangler.jsonc
```

The root page loads bounded cursor pages and creates a new post. Native execution and local D1
both reach `Odroe post 42` after applying the same two SQL files. `Post` and `CreatePost`
remain Dart named records across Flutter, generated RPC, the server handler,
and the complete SQL projection. Odroe generates the record wire codecs—there
is no handwritten map, annotation, or adapter in application code.

Application creation omits the ID and uses one typed `INSERT ... RETURNING` statement. The
database owns the generated ID, and the returned `Post` is the value shown to
the client.

Native SQLite persists at `.odroe/app.sqlite3` by default, and the initializer
adds `.odroe/` to `.gitignore`. Set `ODROE_SQLITE_PATH` to override it; use an
absolute path on writable persistent storage in production. The native entry
applies pending files before listening and preserves existing rows across
restarts. `ODROE_MIGRATIONS_PATH` overrides the default `migrations/` path.
In source and development runs, relative paths resolve from the project process
directory. An owned Native bundle self-locates from its executable before
application creation, so its defaults and relative overrides resolve from the
bundle root regardless of the caller working directory.
The starter's application-owned `odroe.yaml` contains
`sqlite_migrations: migrations`. Native `odroe dev` watches that source and
restarts after a SQL file changes; build and prerender validate the same source.
Use `--sqlite-migrations <path>` only to override one command. A project without
either selection does not make the CLI reserve or guess a generic migration
directory for build or watch.

Migration files must use `NNNN_snake_case.sql`, stay top-level, and only move
forward. Never edit, delete, or rename an applied file. Native SQLite records
the exact SQL string and rejects divergent history; Wrangler owns a separate D1
ledger and cannot detect edited source, so code review and immutable deployment
artifacts remain part of the contract.

For an unchanged generated starter, running the initializer again is safe. An unchanged starter produces no file
changes. The smaller `dart run odroe init` remains available when a product
needs only Document and Router; an untouched small starter can later upgrade
atomically with `--full-stack`.

## Run on Flutter and the native server

Start both the Odroe development server and Flutter Web:

```sh
dart run odroe dev -- -d chrome
```

Open the reported Flutter URL. Infinite Query calls the generated `posts.list`
reference with a page size of 20 and renders `Odroe post 42`. When another page
exists, Load more passes its typed `nextCursor` back to the same function. Enter a title and choose Create;
Mutation calls `posts.create`, invalidates the post-list key, and renders the
database-returned record. Stop the command with Ctrl-C; Odroe drains the
listener before closing the process-owned database. Starting the command again
reopens the same `.odroe/app.sqlite3` file, including posts you created.

To inspect only the semantic server response, run:

```sh
dart run odroe dev --server-only
```

Visit `http://127.0.0.1:3000/`. Development reads `public/` directly and never
reuses stale HTML from `build/web`.

Browser RPC is same-origin. A native device cannot use the browser's relative
origin, so pass the server origin explicitly:

```sh
flutter devices
dart run odroe dev -- -d <device-id> \
  --dart-define=ODROE_RPC_ORIGIN=https://api.example.com
```

The generated `rpc_origin.dart` rejects a missing or malformed native origin at
startup rather than failing on the first request.

## Run the edge path locally

Node 22+ and npm 10.9+ are used only by the project-owned Cloudflare toolchain.
They do not enter the Dart dependency graph, Flutter artifact, or Worker
bundle. Run these commands from `my_app`:

```sh
npm ci
dart run odroe build --no-server web
npm run cloudflare:migrate:local
npm run cloudflare:dev
```

The migration command and Odroe's Cloudflare development runtime both keep
Wrangler local. `cloudflare:dev` regenerates routes, compiles the current
Worker, atomically replaces its server JavaScript, and then starts the
project-local Wrangler. Dart server changes recompile without restarting the
CLI. After a successful start, a failed
generation or compile leaves the last-known-good Worker serving requests until
the source is fixed. An initial route generation or compile failure exits
instead of running an old artifact.

`/` serves the semantic handoff and the same typed cursor-page/create RPC uses the
invocation's `DB` binding. Creating a post exercises D1 `INSERT ... RETURNING`
and refreshes the same typed list. This server-only watch does not rebuild
Flutter Web or static assets; run `build --no-server web` again after UI or
asset changes. Removing `--local` or deploying changes remote state and is a
separate operation.

## Build only what you need

```sh
# Flutter Web plus prerendered HTML; no deployable server
dart run odroe build --no-server web

# Flutter WebAssembly plus the same Document handoff
dart run odroe build --no-server -- web --wasm

# Native server plus the configured SQLite history
dart run odroe build --server-only

# Cloudflare Worker only
dart run odroe build --server-only --server-target cloudflare
```

The Native command publishes the complete bundle root at
`build/odroe/server`: invoke `bin/server` (`bin/server.exe` on Windows) from any
working directory and keep its `lib/` directory beside `bin/`. The owned bundle
marker makes the executable select this root before application creation. A
compiled entry rejects a missing, non-regular, or changed marker before opening
application state. With the configured migration selection, the same root also
contains `migrations/`. A custom Native `--server-artifact` selects this root,
not the `bin/server` file.

`dev` and `build` regenerate route targets automatically. Use
`dart run odroe generate` for a fast explicit compiler check.

Native prerender is isolated from runtime state. The CLI overrides
`ODROE_SQLITE_PATH` for the prerender child with a temporary database and removes
it after the child stops. `odroe.yaml` or `--sqlite-migrations` pins the source
path. The CLI also retains the project working directory for build-time source
resources. The generated starter therefore never reads or writes the local
`.odroe/app.sqlite3` file during a build.

